research/audit/wave14-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-10744-+{"id":"def-real-exponential-function-and-e","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://live.ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-100b-real-analysis-spring-2025/mit18_100b_s25_lec_full.pdf","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_logandexp.html"],"rationale":"The MIT notes use the same globally convergent factorial power series for exp, and Lebl defines e=exp(1). The library packages those two standard conventions in one definition and makes the canonical-natural embedding explicit, so the content is semantically standard but not verbatim from one source. A definition supplies no proof.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave14-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-10745-+{"id":"lem-exponential-series-has-infinite-radius","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://live.ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-100b-real-analysis-spring-2025/mit18_100b_s25_lec_full.pdf"],"rationale":"The MIT notes state that the exponential power series has infinite radius and hence converges for every real argument. The local proof replaces their ratio-test presentation by an explicit geometric-tail comparison.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave14-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-10746-+{"id":"thm-exponential-addition-formula","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"literature-derived","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch10.pdf","https://live.ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-100b-real-analysis-spring-2025/mit18_100b_s25_lec_full.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Proposition 10.24 states E(x)E(y)=E(x+y) for all real x,y and proves it by the same Cauchy-product and binomial-theorem calculation. The library's embedded-factorial notation is only a notational convention.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave14-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-10747-+{"id":"cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch10.pdf","https://math.mit.edu/~stevenj/exponential.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter states E(-x)=1/E(x) and E(x)>0 for every real x immediately after the addition formula; Johnson also derives reciprocal and positivity from the multiplicative equation. The local square argument is an adapted proof.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave14-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-10748-+{"id":"thm-derivative-of-exponential","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch10.pdf","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_analfuncs.html"],"rationale":"Hunter gives E'=E from the factorial series, while Lebl's analytic-functions section gives termwise derivatives and the resulting smoothness of power-series sums. The item combines these into the stronger all-orders identity exp^(m)=exp.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave14-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-10749-+{"id":"thm-exponential-is-strictly-increasing","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_logandexp.html","https://people.math.wisc.edu/~hkeisler/foundations.pdf"],"rationale":"Lebl's exponential proposition states that exp is strictly increasing and differentiable with positive derivative. The local proof uses the mean value theorem rather than Lebl's inverse-log construction.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave14-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log:10750:+{"id":"thm-exponential-limits-and-range","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_logandexp.html","https://odp.library.tamu.edu/math150-2nd-ed/chapter/5-2-properties-and-graphs-of-exponential-functions/"],"rationale":"The sources give the limits 0 and +infinity at the two ends and range (0,infinity). The item restates these together with the elementary bound exp(x)>=1+x and a specific reciprocal proof; that proof is locally assembled.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave14-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log:10751:+{"id":"cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_logandexp.html","https://odp.library.tamu.edu/math150-2nd-ed/chapter/5-2-properties-and-graphs-of-exponential-functions/"],"rationale":"Lebl states that the exponential is a strictly increasing bijection from R to (0,infinity), exactly the corollary's mathematical assertion. The library proof repackages monotonicity, endpoint limits, continuity, and IVT.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave14-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-10752-+{"id":"thm-exponential-beats-every-polynomial","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.mit.edu/~rbm/18-102-Sp16/Chapter4.pdf"],"rationale":"The MIT notes prove that the exponential dominates every fixed polynomial power using a retained factorial-series term. The item includes an arbitrary positive scaling a and spells out both infinity limits, a standard change of variables and reciprocal reformulation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave14-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-10753-+{"id":"thm-exponential-ivp-uniqueness","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch10.pdf","https://www2.math.upenn.edu/~ancoop/3600/section-34.html"],"rationale":"Hunter Proposition 10.25 and the Penn notes characterize exp as the unique differentiable solution of f'=f with f(0)=1. The local integrating-factor proof is a standard adaptation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave14-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log:10754:+{"id":"thm-normalized-exponential-functional-equation","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://people.tamu.edu/~yvorobets/MATH409-2013C/Lect2-07web.pdf","https://math.mit.edu/~stevenj/exponential.pdf"],"rationale":"The Texas A&M notes give uniqueness of the continuous positive multiplicative function normalized at 1, and Johnson derives exponential form from continuity of a nonzero multiplicative solution. The item combines that standard characterization with the differentiability-at-zero normalization F(0)=F'(0)=1, so no one located source has its exact two-part packaging.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave14-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-10755-+{"id":"lem-scaled-binomial-coefficients-converge","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_logandexp.html","https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch10.pdf"],"rationale":"This fixed-k coefficient limit and 0<=product<=1 bound are the standard coefficient calculation underlying binomial proofs of (1+x/n)^n -> e^x. The located sources state that product limit and the binomial machinery, but not this extracted lemma verbatim; the finite-product derivation is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave14-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-10756-+{"id":"thm-exponential-product-limit","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_logandexp.html","https://people.math.wisc.edu/~hkeisler/foundations.pdf"],"rationale":"Lebl Exercise 5.4.5 states e^x=lim_n(1+x/n)^n for every real x, with the same eventual-domain convention implicit in a real sequence. The library supplies a binomial head-tail proof instead of Lebl's logarithmic hint.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave14-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-10757-+{"id":"thm-picard-iterates-for-exponential","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_picard.html","https://www2.math.upenn.edu/~ancoop/3600/section-34.html"],"rationale":"Lebl's Picard example for y'=y, y(0)=1 gives the iterates 1+x+...+x^n/n! and their global limit exp. The item additionally records compact-uniform convergence and the limiting integral equation, so it is a faithful strengthened packaging; its induction and interchange proof adapt the standard construction.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
